## opencode specifics

- **Skills** — the workflow phases (`using-rolepod` router + `write-spec`,
  `write-plan`, `implement-plan`, `debug-issue`, `check-work`,
  `review-code`, `finish-work`, `simplify-code`, `manage-context`) install
  at `~/.config/opencode/skills/<name>/` in opencode's native SKILL.md
  format and auto-trigger from their `description:`. The S/T/F gates and CI
  lane policy live inside those phase skills — invoke the phase skill
  rather than reciting gates here.
- **Agents** — 16 specialists install at `~/.config/opencode/agents/*.md`
  as subagents. opencode auto-invokes them by description or via `@<name>`
  mention. Each agent file is self-contained — it carries its own agent
  protocol, including the subagent commit ban.
- **Hooks** — a best-effort plugin (`plugins/rolepod.js`) handles session
  locking (cross-CLI sibling detection via `~/.rolepod/session-locks/`)
  and the post-compaction re-anchor nudge. The pre-commit / subagent-commit
  gates are NOT hook-blocked on opencode — they are skill-enforced only;
  the Lead must hold them as doctrine. **Enforcement tier: doctrine-only** —
  never report a gate as mechanically enforced on this CLI.
- **Cross-family review** — opencode is multi-provider: switching the
  provider of a reviewer agent can satisfy the cross-model adversarial
  pass without leaving the CLI. Record the actual family used — a reviewer
  sharing the Lead's family is still `vertical — same family`.
- **Tier classes on a big catalog** (OpenRouter etc.) — rolepod's cheap /
  balanced / strong are CLASSES, never model names. Map them ONCE per
  session onto the models the user has OPTED INTO (configured providers /
  models they already pay for) — never the full aggregator catalog:
  exposure is not authorization, and a rung costlier than anything the
  user configured is a cost decision to surface, not a silent dispatch.
  Cheap = small/fast, balanced = mid flagship, strong = top reasoning
  model of the set (any family); the set's ceiling is this machine's
  apex. Strong resolving below opus-class → run the review anyway and
  record the depth cap as a LIMITATION in the report. Stay consistent —
  churning models mid-task wastes cache and muddies review provenance.
  Cannot classify a model → treat it as balanced and say so.
